## Founding & History
Ghost Rock Wines was established in 2001 by Cate and Colin Arnold on a property east of Devonport, Tasmania, which initially had a small number of vines. Some sources indicate the vineyard, originally known as Patrick's Creek vineyard, was purchased in 1999 and had been planted in 1989. The Arnolds are considered pioneers of the Cradle Coast (or North West) wine region, establishing the first winery in the area. In 2015, their son Justin and his wife Alicia joined the family business, taking full ownership in 2017. The property has expanded significantly from an initial 3 hectares to over 30 hectares of vines.

## People
The current owners of Ghost Rock Wines are Justin Arnold and his wife Alicia Peardon, who took over the business in 2017. Justin Arnold is responsible for the winemaking, having gained experience at Coldstream Hills, Devil's Lair, and Etude in California's Carneros Creek. Alicia Peardon manages the Cellar Door and Eatery. Izaak Perkins is the Viticulture Manager, overseeing the farming of the Arnold family's property. Sierra Blair is also noted as a winemaker for Ghost Rock Wines.

## Vineyard & Site
Ghost Rock Wines is located in Tasmania's North West wine region, also known as the Cradle Coast, approximately 15 minutes east of Devonport and 4 kilometres from Bass Strait. The vineyard spans between 26 and 30 hectares across seven blocks, with plantings dating from 2004 to 2018. The site experiences a cool, maritime climate with a large diurnal range and moderate temperatures, leading to extended ripening periods. The soils are described as fertile loamy clay, with some volcanic soils.

Pinot Noir is the predominant varietal, accounting for 45% of plantings across 12 clones. Chardonnay and Pinot Gris each make up 20% of the plantings, with Chardonnay featuring eight clones. Other varietals include Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, and Pinot Meunier. The farming approach is sustainable, with an end goal of organic certification. Kelly's Block, planted in 2016, is farmed according to organic principles, though not yet certified. Across the other six blocks, no pesticides are used, and herbicides have been replaced by manual methods like under-vine cultivation and controlled grazing by sheep. Vine prunings are mulched, and machine operations are being phased out in favor of hand tending to reduce soil compaction. Biodegradable ties and steel posts are also used.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Cellar Door at Ghost Rock Wines offers a personalized tasting experience overlooking the original vineyard and Bass Strait. Visitors can choose between guided or self-guided tastings from their portfolio of 19 wines across three ranges. A guided cellar door experience of five wines costs $15, with the tasting complimentary if wine is purchased to take away. Bookings are highly recommended, especially during peak seasons or for larger groups. The Cellar Door is generally open 7 days a week from 11 am to 5 pm during sunny months, and 5 days a week (Wednesday to Sunday) from 11 am to 5 pm during less sunny months. The team provides insights into the winemaking philosophy, the Ghost Rock legend, and the history of the Cradle Coast wine region.

## Food & Hospitality
Ghost Rock Wines features an on-site eatery that offers a menu of wholesome lunches, focusing on local Tasmanian produce and guided by seasonal availability. The food philosophy mirrors their wine philosophy, emphasizing minimal intervention to let the quality of the produce shine. The kitchen is open from 11 am to 3:30 pm. Tables typically have a two-hour sitting time, and reservations are recommended, especially for lunch. The maximum lunch booking size is 8 people; for larger groups, set menus are available, requiring prior email inquiry. The winery also offers on-site accommodation called The Vineyard House, a three-bedroom home with views of the vineyard and Bass Strait, providing a base for exploring the region.

## Awards & Recognition
Ghost Rock Wines has received 5-star recognition from the Halliday Wine Companion. Their 2024 Estate Pinot Noir was judged the best Pinot Noir at a recent event. The winery is described as an "award-winning" establishment.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Ghost Rock Wines is committed to sustainable farming practices with an end goal of organic certification. They are in the process of converting blocks to organic or "sustainable farming". Kelly's Block, planted in 2016, is farmed according to organic principles, though it is not yet certified. Across other blocks, pesticides are not used, and herbicides have been replaced by manual methods like under-vine cultivation and controlled grazing of sheep. They also mulch vine prunings, are phasing out machine operations for hand tending to reduce soil compaction, and use biodegradable ties and steel posts. Ghost Rock Wines participates in the Tasmanian VinØ Program, a sustainability framework for Tasmanian wine businesses. They hold Quality Tourism Accreditation and Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Ghost Rock Wines is 1055 Port Sorell Road, Northdown, Tasmania, 7307, Australia. It is located approximately 15 minutes from Devonport (where the Spirit of Tasmania docks) and about an hour's drive from Launceston and Cradle Mountain. The phone number is (03) 6428 4005. Their website is www.ghostrock.com.au. They maintain a presence on social media platforms like Instagram and Facebook. The winery offers accommodation at The Vineyard House. Ghost Rock Wines participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme, allowing visitors to ship purchased wines home. The venue is family-friendly and offers alfresco/outdoor dining, with some pet-friendly options (enquire directly). They also cater to people with various access needs, including those using wheelchairs and those with allergies or intolerances.

## Notable / Distinctive
Ghost Rock Wines is recognized as a pioneering winery in Tasmania's North West (Cradle Coast) wine region, having established the first commercial winery and vineyard in an area that was previously undeveloped for viticulture. The Arnold family almost single-handedly created this wine region. The locals even refer to current owner Justin Arnold as "The Mayor" of the Cradle Coast wine region. The winery's commitment to growing, vinifying, and bottling all their wines on-site was once a rarity in Tasmania. The name "Ghost Rock" itself is derived from a local legend about a rock where "ghosts" were claimed to appear, which was a popular prank spot in the late 19th century. The winery's location, just 4 kilometers from Bass Strait, provides a unique cool, maritime climate that influences their distinct wine styles.

